  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Shutout losses have a weight the box score can't hold. Thirteen strikeouts and four hits. That's not a game, that's silence.",
    "what_this_means": "The Padres played tonight like a team hoping the pitching would be good enough to win without them. It was. Wandy Peralta threw a clean inning. Griffin Canning kept the Dodgers to one run across four innings. Kyle Hart added two scoreless. Mason Miller closed it. None of it mattered because the offense never arrived. Two runners in scoring position across nine innings, zero runs converted. They never recovered from trailing after the third, and by the time anyone looked ready to swing, the game was already decided. The story wasn't the pitching. It was the bats refusing to show up when there was still time.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is inconsistency wearing a new shape. Not a slump where the lineup looks overmatched. This is the bats going missing entirely while competent pitching waits for help that doesn't come. That gap. Between what the pitching gives them and what the offense demands. Is widening.",
